Service vs Filter Boundaries
Domain-specific exceptions, contextual translation, and keeping business meaning
out of global infrastructure code

Phase 13 - Professional Error Handling
Volume 04 - Professional Backend

Architecture milestone
service understands use case

↓

exception carries meaning

↓

filter formats HTTP

Phase 13 creates one professional exception boundary and a correlation story that spans client response and server logs.

Create application-specific exceptions only when they
add meaning
NestJS already has NotFoundException and ConflictException. TaskFlow subclasses them only when a stable
application code or reusable domain message earns its place.

import {
ConflictException,
NotFoundException,
} from "@nestjs/common";

export class TaskNotFoundException extends NotFoundException {
constructor(taskId: string) {
super({
code: "TASK_NOT_FOUND",
message: `Task ${taskId} was not found.`,
});
}
}

export class ProjectNotFoundException extends NotFoundException {
constructor(projectId: string) {
super({
code: "PROJECT_NOT_FOUND",
message: `Project ${projectId} was not found.`,
});
}
}

export class ResourceConflictException extends ConflictException {
constructor(message: string, code = "RESOURCE_CONFLICT") {
super({ code, message });
}
}

TasksService now communicates domain meaning
The service decides that a missing Task is a Task-domain event, not merely a Prisma event. That keeps the
public API independent from which ORM we use.

import { Injectable } from "@nestjs/common";
import { Prisma } from "../generated/prisma/client";
import { PrismaService } from "../prisma/prisma.service";
import {
ProjectNotFoundException,
TaskNotFoundException,
} from "../common/errors/application.exceptions";
import {
CreateTaskDto,
UpdateTaskDto,
} from "./tasks.schemas";

@Injectable()
export class TasksService {
constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaService) {}

findAll() {
return this.prisma.task.findMany({
orderBy: { createdAt: "desc" },
});
}

async findOne(id: string) {
const task = await this.prisma.task.findUnique({
where: { id },
});

if (!task) {
throw new TaskNotFoundException(id);
}

return task;
}

async create(input: CreateTaskDto) {
const project = await this.prisma.project.findUnique({
where: { id: input.projectId },
select: { id: true },
});

if (!project) {
throw new ProjectNotFoundException(input.projectId);

}

return this.prisma.task.create({
data: input,
});
}

async update(id: string, input: UpdateTaskDto) {
try {
return await this.prisma.task.update({
where: { id },
data: input,
});
} catch (error) {
this.rethrowMissingTask(error, id);
}
}

async remove(id: string) {
try {
await this.prisma.task.delete({
where: { id },
});
} catch (error) {
this.rethrowMissingTask(error, id);
}
}

private rethrowMissingTask(error: unknown, id: string): never {
if (
error instanceof Prisma.PrismaClientKnownRequestError &&
error.code === "P2025"
) {
throw new TaskNotFoundException(id);
}

throw error;
}
}

Responsibility boundaries
Layer                                  Owns

Zod / pipe                             Request shape validity

TasksService                           Task/Project existence and business meaning

Prisma                                 Persistence operations and database protocol

ApiExceptionFilter                     HTTP serialization, safe fallback, final logging

Anti-pattern: filter contains business rules
// Avoid turning the global filter into this:
if (exception.code === "P2025" && request.url.startsWith("/tasks")) {
// infer Task business meaning from URL here...
}

Architecture rule

The global filter should not reverse-engineer domain context from routes or database metadata. Services know the
use case. Filters know HTTP error serialization.

Anti-pattern: swallowing unknown errors
If a service catches unknown Error and turns it into BadRequestException, a programming bug can
masquerade as a client mistake. Catch only failures you understand and can meaningfully translate. Re-throw
the rest.

