Client Error Consumption & Debugging
Parse the stable envelope in Next.js and distinguish API failures from transport
failures

Phase 13 - Professional Error Handling
Volume 04 - Professional Backend

Architecture milestone
fetch

↓

network vs HTTP response

↓

ApiClientError

↓

UI recovery

Phase 13 creates one professional exception boundary and a correlation story that spans client response and server logs.

Frontend should consume the contract, not framework
accidents
Next.js should not need to know whether NestJS, Zod, Prisma, PostgreSQL, or Fastify produced the failure. It
should parse TaskFlow's stable API error response.

export type ApiErrorResponse = {
statusCode: number;
code: string;
message: string;
requestId: string;
timestamp: string;
path: string;
details?: string[] | Record<string, unknown>;
};

export class ApiClientError extends Error {
constructor(
public readonly statusCode: number,
public readonly code: string,
message: string,
public readonly requestId?: string,
public readonly details?: ApiErrorResponse["details"],
) {
super(message);
}
}

export async function throwIfNotOk(response: Response) {
if (response.ok) return;

let payload: ApiErrorResponse | undefined;

try {
payload = (await response.json()) as ApiErrorResponse;
} catch {
// The server may have returned a non-JSON infrastructure error.
}

throw new ApiClientError(
response.status,
payload?.code ?? "HTTP_ERROR",
payload?.message ?? "The request failed.",
payload?.requestId,
payload?.details,
);
}

Typical UI decisions
Error condition                                 Possible UI behavior

BAD_REQUEST + details                           Show field/form feedback or a request summary

TASK_NOT_FOUND                                  Show “Task no longer exists” and navigate back

UNAUTHORIZED later                              Prompt login / refresh session

FORBIDDEN later                                 Explain missing permission

CONFLICT                                        Explain stale/duplicate state and offer recovery

INTERNAL_ERROR                                  Show generic failure + requestId for support

Network error is not the same as API error

If fetch cannot reach the server, there may be no HTTP status, no JSON envelope, and no requestId. That is a
transport failure. If the API returns 404/409/500, fetch receives an HTTP response and the TaskFlow error
parser can process the body.

Figure 1 - Client failure split
fetch() call

↓

No HTTP response -> transport/network error

↓

HTTP response received

↓

response.ok false -> parse ApiErrorResponse

↓

throw ApiClientError

The UI can now distinguish connectivity from server-declared application failures.

Debug with requestId first
When an INTERNAL_ERROR reaches the browser, show a short message and the requestId. Do not dump
stack traces into the UI. During development, the same ID should be visible in the Nest terminal log so you can
correlate the failure quickly.

